    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Who should choose ServerHub?</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">ServerHub fits buyers who want a <strong>long-running hosting brand with verifiable datacenters, 10G-ready networking, and a choice of Pure SSD or SSD-Cached tiers</strong>. The 9-DC footprint covers the US and Europe cleanly, and the IPv6-native stack plus 100+ direct peering relationships (including Amazon, Facebook, Cox, Softlayer) make it a strong fit for IPv6-first deployments, multi-region fail-over within the US + EU, and workloads that benefit from RAID-10 storage with full root access.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8">It is not the right fit if you need always-on in-line DDoS scrubbing, an Asia-Pacific or South American POP, or absolute lowest entry sticker price. The $5/mo Starter ships only 1 GB RAM which is tight beyond a single small site, and the company does not advertise per-hour billing like Vultr or DigitalOcean. For cheapest-of-the-cheap unmanaged Linux, RackNerd at $1.99/mo still wins; ServerHub competes on 23-year track record, multi-region US/EU footprint, and a Juniper + Cisco backbone, not on lowest dollar.</p>
